Operator 2026-06-28: "instead of injecting into the context, have we tried giving the LLM
an MCP server that allows it to interrogate and interact with the live agent, BFS, any TRMs,
and any generated game code?"

9-agent adversarial audit (workflow arc-llm-tooluse-mcp-not-done-before-audit):
- GENUINELY UNTRIED in-repo: zero tools=/tool_choice/function_calling/ReAct in
  python/carnot/agentic/; live LLM is a stateless one-shot proposer (LocalGGUFProposer
  .induce/.complete_text, single raw /completion POST) fired ~once/game on stall;
  BFS/env/TRM never exposed as callable tools; TRM retired+absent; the existing
  carnot/mcp/server.py is the INVERSE wiring (verify tools for external clients, not ARC).
- Closest precedents DISTINCT: arc_llm_reinduction.py verify->refactor loop is a Python
  for-loop injecting counterexamples (context-injection, not LLM tool-driving); IGE
  cell-selector is an off-by-default passive ranker (nulled).
- Adversarially REFUTED on "would it beat context-injection": binding wall
  (WALL_IS_HIDDEN_STATE exp4914) is information-theoretic; LLM-with-tools only observes
  frame + own actions (both proven insufficient: exp4914, exp4893). Weak legal 9B +
  squared-RHAE + 8h cap compound. Honest prior ~12%.
- Recommended cheapest-decisive probe documented (offline frontier-dev tool-loop A/B with
  a falsifiable held-out first-win gate; zero extra env actions) before any 9B/Kaggle build.

Captured so the planner neither re-audits nor naively builds it without the wall caveat.

